WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:29.980
80% of 194 patients had lasting knee pain relief after genicular artery embolization. This is Planetarian Daily, episode 213. Researchers sent gelatin micro spheres that resorb quickly through a thin catheter, according to science daily. The particles shut off abnormal vessels feeding the inflamed joint. That step cuts the inflammation and nerve growth linked to chronic pain, while normal knee circulation stays intact. Super selective targeting of small arteries replaces any broad

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.520
blockage of the joint's blood supply. Everyone treated had already exhausted other options short of

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:42.080
surgery. Florian Nima Fleckinstein at Charite University's Medicine Berlin noted that the method

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:48.160
may alter disease progression by modulating the neurovascular environment. osteoarthritis involves

00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:52.940
cartilage deterioration plus inflammation and abnormal vessel growth in the synobium.

00:00:53.640 --> 00:00:59.460
Genicular artery embolization aims at that hypervascularity directly. The microspheres dissolve

00:00:59.460 --> 00:01:05.200
within hours, pairing precise delivery with a temporary blockage. That design differs from

00:01:05.200 --> 00:01:09.920
earlier permanent particle approaches and may reduce risks of lasting vascular changes.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:15.880
The work was published in radiology, whether the same particles can slow cartilage loss itself

00:01:15.880 --> 00:01:21.280
or mains under investigation. How consistently the technique can be performed outside specialized

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:27.208
centers is still an open question. The human cell atlas consortium is preparing to scale from

00:01:27.208 --> 00:01:33.248
millions of cells toward 100 billion nature reports. Integrated atlases for lung, liver,

00:01:33.488 --> 00:01:39.108
and other organs already exist from single cell and spatial methods. New spatial transcriptomics

00:01:39.108 --> 00:01:44.648
approaches now map thousands of RNAs along with proteins and metabolites while preserving location.

00:01:45.328 --> 00:01:50.608
Standards for data quality and cell annotation were set early so data sets stay comparable across

00:01:50.608 --> 00:01:56.588
labs. The next phase has to handle orders of magnitude more cells while folding in those multi-modal

00:01:56.588 --> 00:02:02.248
measurements. The project began in 2016 with the goal of mapping every cell in the human body,

00:02:02.748 --> 00:02:07.948
and droplet-based sequencing plus SmartSeq 2 made routine counts of tens of thousands of cells

00:02:07.948 --> 00:02:14.511
possible that same year. A DNA-based recorder now tracks cell decisions during embryonic development,

00:02:14.751 --> 00:02:20.951
according to Fizz.org. The system edits DNA in a cumulative way so successive molecular events are

00:02:20.951 --> 00:02:26.671
logged inside developing cells. Lineage and state changes are captured at single cell resolution

00:02:26.671 --> 00:02:31.251
across the embryo. The temporal record can be read after development has finished.

00:02:31.711 --> 00:02:35.091
The recorder functions like a flight data logger for cellular history.

00:02:36.078 --> 00:02:40.578
Second language pronunciation improved faster when listeners could hear tiny sound differences,

00:02:41.098 --> 00:02:47.158
work in the Journal of Experimental Psychology found. Fizz.org described participants who scored higher

00:02:47.158 --> 00:02:53.218
on auditory precision tasks, acquiring new pronunciation more accurately than peers with lower scores.

00:02:53.218 --> 00:02:59.138
Find sound discrimination scores also lined up with quicker mastery of novel phonetic categories.

00:02:59.518 --> 00:03:04.898
The experiments tested how listeners tell apart subtle acoustic differences in those new categories.

00:03:05.498 --> 00:03:09.978
The result identifies a measurable perceptual skill that supports language learning.

00:03:10.903 --> 00:03:16.483
Ludwig Maximilian University experiments found people assigned AI less consciousness than humans,

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:20.323
even when the described behavior matches, according to Science Daily.

00:03:20.323 --> 00:03:25.323
Nearly 1,100 participants judged descriptions of responsive agents.

00:03:25.963 --> 00:03:31.383
Characters labeled as AI drew lower consciousness ratings than humans, giving the exact same

00:03:31.383 --> 00:03:33.163
responses to sounds or emotions.

00:03:33.903 --> 00:03:38.623
The gap held even when the most responsive AI was set against the least responsive human.

00:03:39.223 --> 00:03:44.183
Louis Langeon, the lead author, said participants clearly separate the term consciousness from

00:03:44.183 --> 00:03:45.323
observable behavior.

00:03:45.323 --> 00:03:50.774
Here, most people assume matching reactions should earn an artificial agent the same consciousness

00:03:50.774 --> 00:03:51.874
score as a person.

00:03:52.314 --> 00:03:55.734
The split showed up only when that specific word was on the page.

00:03:56.374 --> 00:03:59.754
Judgments of awareness sat much closer for the two kinds of agents.

00:04:00.354 --> 00:04:05.374
Higher responsiveness lifted consciousness ratings for both humans and machines, yet the

00:04:05.374 --> 00:04:08.114
human baseline stayed higher at every level tested.

00:04:08.734 --> 00:04:14.134
In daily use of chatbots or voice assistance, talk that credits in AI with noticing events

00:04:14.134 --> 00:04:16.474
or picking up tone often feels natural.

00:04:17.074 --> 00:04:19.874
Talk that credits inner experience still meets resistance.

00:04:20.334 --> 00:04:22.754
The protocol used short descriptions of agents

00:04:22.754 --> 00:04:24.794
that reacted to environmental sounds

00:04:24.794 --> 00:04:27.274
or recognized other people's emotional states.

00:04:27.754 --> 00:04:30.694
One group read versions labeled as artificial agents.

00:04:31.234 --> 00:04:33.674
Another group read the same scenes labeled as human.

00:04:34.334 --> 00:04:36.354
Each participant then rated how conscious

00:04:36.354 --> 00:04:37.894
or aware the character seemed.

00:04:38.674 --> 00:04:40.934
Responsiveness moved the numbers in both groups,

00:04:41.374 --> 00:04:43.454
but the consciousness gap stayed consistent

00:04:43.454 --> 00:04:48.654
no matter how active the agent looked. Awareness ratings followed nearly parallel patterns for

00:04:48.654 --> 00:04:54.414
humans and AI. Consciousness carries implications of subjective experience that awareness does not.

00:04:55.154 --> 00:05:00.354
That wording split helps explain why ordinary descriptions of machine behavior rarely get treated as

00:05:00.354 --> 00:05:06.962
equal to a human mind. Those results were published in the journal Cognition. An AI-assisted search

00:05:06.962 --> 00:05:11.562
found nearly 10,000 overlooked reactions for turning carbon dioxide into fuel,

00:05:11.562 --> 00:05:16.662
approval, according to Science Daily. Machine learning mapped reaction networks that conventional

00:05:16.662 --> 00:05:22.622
models had missed. A previously underappreciated hydrogen pathway turned up in that map and

00:05:22.622 --> 00:05:27.262
could guide more efficient catalyst design. Predictions of carbon dioxide conversion

00:05:27.262 --> 00:05:32.362
improved substantially once the hidden roots were included. The study targeted reactions

00:05:32.362 --> 00:05:38.182
that convert carbon dioxide into usable fuels and chemicals. The search also uncovered shortcuts

00:05:38.182 --> 00:05:43.402
that traditional kinetic models had bypassed. Fossils on the Isle of White belonged to the

00:05:43.402 --> 00:05:50.462
first European Megaraptor in Dinosaur on record, according to Fizz.org. A UCL-led team described

00:05:50.462 --> 00:05:56.322
a long-clod carnivore, roughly the size of a large tiger. The specimen sits in the Megaraptor

00:05:56.322 --> 00:06:01.702
and group of agile, two-legged predators. The find adds to the picture of dinosaur distribution

00:06:01.702 --> 00:06:08.087
in the early Cretaceous. Elongated claws fit the animal's predatory lifestyle. Researchers

00:06:08.087 --> 00:06:12.767
identified a new dinosaur species from Zimbabwe that lived about 210 million

00:06:12.767 --> 00:06:15.047
years ago, according to Science Daily.

00:06:15.527 --> 00:06:19.667
Its setting was a prehistoric environment cut off from other southern African sites

00:06:19.667 --> 00:06:21.187
by ancient climate differences.

00:06:21.907 --> 00:06:25.807
Regional dinosaur populations appear to have evolved in relative isolation.

00:06:26.527 --> 00:06:30.907
The result challenges earlier assumptions of uniform fauna's across the continent in

00:06:30.907 --> 00:06:31.487
that period.

00:06:32.087 --> 00:06:36.927
The specimen dates to the late triassic and helps reconstruct local ecological conditions.

00:06:36.927 --> 00:06:47.070
BaraLon University researchers mapped three-dimensional chemical composition 43 times faster with a patterned X-ray method, according to Fizz.org.

00:06:47.730 --> 00:06:52.150
Required measurements dropped by more than 97% while the map stayed complete.

00:06:52.850 --> 00:06:58.270
Structured illumination pulls composition data from far fewer projections than conventional tomography.

00:06:58.890 --> 00:07:03.550
The method allows faster, lower dose analysis of material makeup inside objects.

00:07:03.550 --> 00:07:08.350
It is built for non-destructive characterization of what those objects contain.

00:07:09.171 --> 00:07:13.791
A computational model now guides lighting meant to aid color vision in older adults,

00:07:14.051 --> 00:07:15.331
according to Fizz.org.

00:07:15.871 --> 00:07:20.231
The model accounts for age-related changes in the eyes ability to discriminate colors.

00:07:20.951 --> 00:07:24.811
Lighting designs based on it can improve color perception for older adults

00:07:24.811 --> 00:07:27.951
without changing how a scene looks to people with typical vision.

00:07:28.651 --> 00:07:32.871
Known optical and neural changes that come with aging are built into the calculations.

00:07:32.871 --> 00:07:38.271
The tool predicts how specific wavelength combinations shift discrimination thresholds

00:07:38.271 --> 00:07:39.271
across age groups.

00:07:40.294 --> 00:07:44.654
Lithium and drinking water was linked to higher congenital heart arrhythmia risk in an

00:07:44.654 --> 00:07:45.994
analysis in JCI.

00:07:46.674 --> 00:07:51.434
Higher lithium concentrations in tap water were associated with increased incidence of wolf

00:07:51.434 --> 00:07:52.774
Parkinson-white syndrome.

00:07:53.334 --> 00:07:57.194
The work combined population-level exposure data with clinical records.

00:07:57.774 --> 00:08:02.174
Trace environmental lithium sits among factors that can influence cardiac development.

00:08:02.174 --> 00:08:05.914
The arrhythmia involves an extra electrical pathway in the heart.

00:08:06.897 --> 00:08:11.477
Dryer Texas rivers showed simpler food webs and higher animal productivity, according

00:08:11.477 --> 00:08:12.437
to Fizz.org.

00:08:13.177 --> 00:08:15.737
Researchers compared river sites that differ in rainfall.

00:08:16.437 --> 00:08:21.337
Reduced flow lined up with fewer species interactions and greater biomass of certain animals.

00:08:21.977 --> 00:08:26.297
Drought appears to alter energy flow through aquatic ecosystems in consistent ways.

00:08:26.997 --> 00:08:30.437
Long-term monitoring across multiple rivers supported that pattern.

00:08:30.437 --> 00:08:36.017
Under drier conditions, the simpler webs still moved more energy into higher trophic levels.

00:08:36.968 --> 00:08:41.728
Before we go, planned follow-up experiments will test whether repeated exposure to highly

00:08:41.728 --> 00:08:46.548
responsive AI narrows the consciousness gap across cultures and age groups.

